      "text": "The function $G(x) = |x+1|-|x-1|$ is defined for all real numbers $x$ because the expressions inside the absolute value signs are always positive or zero. $G(x) = |x-1| = |-(x+1)|$, so our function $G$ is even in the sense that the graph of $G$ is symmetric about the x-axis. (That is, if $(-x_1)$ and $(-x_2)$ are values for which $G(x_1) = G(x_2)$, then so are $(x_1)$ and $(x_2)$. Therefore, we only need to worry about values of $x$ between $-1$ and $1$, or equivalently, we must examine the graph of $G$ when $-1\\le x \\le 1$.\nAbsolute values $|x+1|$ and $|x-1|$ take values in $(0, \\infty)$ when $-1 \\le x \\le 1$, and where $x< -1$ or $x>1$, the values of $|x+1|$ and $|x-1|$ are always negative. We have three critical values for $G(x)$ in the interval $-1\\le x \\le 1$:\n$\\bullet$ When $x=-1$, $G(-1) = |-1+1|-|-1-1| = 0-2 = -2$.\n$\\bullet$ When $x=0$, $G(0) = |0+1|-|0-1| = 1-1 = 0$.\n$\\bullet$ When $x=1$, $G(1) = |1+1|-|1-1| = 2-0 = 2$.\nSince $G(x)$ is continuous $-1 \\le x \\le 1$ and Lipschitz continuous in the interval, it is differentiable in the interior of $-1\\le x \\le 1$. Therefore, we may use the mean value theorem to know the sign of $G'(x)$-that is, whether or not $G'(x)$ takes negative or positive values. Since $G'(x) = 2|x|$, we find that either $G'(x) = 0$ or $G'(x)$ takes values in $(-\\infty, 0)$ or $(0, \\infty)$ in $(0, \\infty)$. Since the critical values we found here, $-2$, $0$, and $2$, in this order, lie on the graph of $G$, we are assured that there are no critical points (and thus no local minima or maxima). Therefore, $G(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-1 \\le x \\le 1$, and its range is $(-2, 2)$.",
